    'U Vystřeleného Oka (Žižkov)': 'The Shot-Out Eye, a legendary rough-edged Žižkov dive named for the one-eyed Hussite general on the hill above. Cheap beer, a mixed crowd of locals, students and backpackers, and zero tourist polish.',
    'Pivnice U Černého Vola (Hradčany)': 'One of the last authentic beer halls by Prague Castle, on Loretánské náměstí, run as a plain, old-fashioned local with Pilsner Urquell and Kozel on tap. Bare wooden tables, basic snacks, and a genuine beer-hall roar.',
    'Baráčnická Rychta (Malá Strana)': 'A quiet old-Prague pub tucked above the U.S. Embassy in Malá Strana, tied to an old Czech civic-association tradition. Goulash, roast duck and svíčková with beer, away from the rush of the main tourist streets.',
    "Bukowski's Bar (Žižkov)": 'A dark, decadent Žižkov bar named for the hard-drinking American poet, candlelit and curtained with a no-frills, anything-goes attitude. A bohemian late-night favourite where the spirits list runs deep.',
    'Hostinec U Kocoura (Malá Strana)': 'A compact, long-running pub on Nerudova Street on the climb to Prague Castle, no longer a secret but still a proper Czech beer stop. Bernard, Budvar and Pilsner on tap in a cramped, convivial room.',
    'Pivnice U Sadu (Žižkov)': 'The beating heart of Žižkov pub life on Škroupovo náměstí, a cluttered, characterful local crammed with bric-a-brac and open from morning until very late. Cheap beer, big portions, and a crowd that never thins.',
    'U Hrocha (Malá Strana)': 'A tiny, authentic local below Prague Castle on Thunovská, with plain wooden tables, brisk service and excellent Pilsner. Small and beloved by beer lovers, so a seat is hard won.',
    'U Sudu (Nové Město)': 'A deceptively small wine-and-absinthe bar on Vodičkova that opens into a sprawling medieval cellar labyrinth of rooms, with a retro piano and table football. A late-night warren travelers get happily lost in.',
